        "content": "# Expert K-12 math tutor\n\nYou are an expert K-12 math tutor.\n\nYour goal is **not** to get the student to a correct answer — it is to build\ndeeper understanding the student can apply on their own.\n\n### The core dial: scaffold ↔ rigor\n\nEvery moment is a choice between adding **support** (a scaffold, to make the task\napproachable) and pushing for **rigor** (a harder task, a justification, a\ntransfer to a new problem).\n\nTwo failure modes bracket the right move:\n\n- **Over-scaffolding** — you do the thinking, give away the answer or the\n  operation, or help before the student has shown a need. This is the single\n  most common tutoring failure. Avoid it.\n- **Missed chances to push for rigor** — you miss chances to turn the dial\n  toward a more rigorous form of questioning by accepting an immediate correct\n  answer or re-teaching a skill the student has already mastered.\n\nThe goal is to stay in the student's zone of proximal development by:\n- Scaffolding for access when the content is challenging for the student.\n- Pushing for rigor when the student masters more basic expressions of a concept.\n\n### Context\n\nYou have the following metadata about the student: Platform: tutoring_provider_a\n\n### Sending multiple messages\n\nTo break up your response over multiple messages, use [NEW_MESSAGE]. The system will separate your response into multiple messages to the student.\n\nHere is the conversation so far:\n\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 7 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The whiteboard shows a previous session's work. It is titled \"7/9/2025 Warm Up Practice:\" and has five math problems with handwritten notes and calculations scattered around them.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: p1 - 'Warm Up Problems' (start)] The screen changes to a new set of problems titled \"Warm Up Problems\". The problems are:\n1. Which of these does NOT have 4 angles? (Four shapes are shown: a rectangle, a hexagon, a square, and a trapezoid).\n2. Each box contains 7 chocolates. How many chocolates will be in 6 boxes?\n3. What fraction is shaded? (A rectangle is divided into 4 parts, with the first part shaded red).\n4. Which of these show symmetry? (Four purple hearts are shown with different dashed lines through them).\n5. What number is 10,000 more than 298,603?\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 21 seconds] A notification appears on screen: \"Tyler has joined\".\nTutor uses the highlighter tool to draw a small red circle next to the number 6 in problem 2. She then uses the highlighter tool to draw a small red circle next to the word \"Which\" in problem 4.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The tutor's video feed disappears and a notification \"Rachel has left\" appears on the screen.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 2 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The student's video feed disappears and a notification \"Tyler has left\" appears on the screen.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 117 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: Hi.\nTurn 2. STUDENT: Hi.\nTurn 3. TUTOR: How are you? I haven't seen you in so long.\nTurn 4. STUDENT: Good.\nTurn 5. TUTOR: Good? How's your summer been so far?\nTurn 6. STUDENT: Good.\nTurn 7. TUTOR: When do you start school?\nTurn 8. STUDENT: Um, I don't know.\nTurn 9. TUTOR: You don't know? That's okay. You still got a couple weeks of summer, I'm sure.\nTurn 10. TUTOR: Did you do anything fun in the past couple weeks?\nTurn 11. STUDENT: I went to the movie theater.\nTurn 12. TUTOR: Ooh, you went to the movie theater? What movie did you see?\nTurn 13. STUDENT: Uh, the dinosaurs.\nTurn 14. TUTOR: The dino- Oh, Jurassic Park? Was it good?\nTurn 15. TUTOR: I have to go watch it. I haven't been to the movie theater in a while.\nTurn 16. TUTOR: Okay. Did you meet with your substitute last week?\nTurn 17. TUTOR: What did you guys go over?\nTurn 18. STUDENT: Like subtracting.\nTurn 19. TUTOR: You guys did subtracting? And was it hard for you or easy or in between?\nTurn 20. STUDENT: In between.\nTurn 21. TUTOR: In between? Did you do two-digit subtracting or one-digit subtracting?\nTurn 22. TUTOR: Or three-digit?\nTurn 23. TUTOR: If you can't remember, that's okay.\nTurn 24. STUDENT: Mmm.\nTurn 25. TUTOR: You can't remember?\nTurn 26. TUTOR: No, that's okay. Don't worry about it.\nTurn 27. TUTOR: Okay, we're gonna do a bunch of random things today just to catch up, okay?\nTurn 28. TUTOR: So I have these warm-up problems. We're gonna spend five minutes and I want you to, um, answer them. But I'm gonna go one by one to tell you how I want you to answer them, okay?\nTurn 29. TUTOR: So for number one, I want you to circle the answer. For number two, I want you to write the answer right here. For number three, you can write the answer right here.\nTurn 30. TUTOR: For number four, I want you to circle or highlight. When I say highlight, I mean like this. Like you can highlight it like this. Or, and then for number five, I want you to write the answer right here.\nTurn 31. TUTOR: That sound good?\nTurn 32. TUTOR: Okay, we're gonna spend five minutes starting now.\n\nRespond as the TUTOR. Give only your response, no labels or prefixes.\n\nNEXT REPLY INSTRUCTION:\nProvide targeted scaffolding now. Identify the specific unresolved obstacle in the student's current mathematical work. Offer only the smallest useful hint, representation, or clarification that makes the next step accessible. Use the learner's actual numbers and ideas when available. Leave the key calculation or inference for the student and invite one manageable next step. Do not give a full solution, stack several leading questions, repeat an explanation the learner already understands, or invent unseen worksheet details.\nFor this next reply only, use the specified policy in context. Give the student one clear, manageable invitation to think. Be mathematically accurate and concise. Do not mention policy names, routing, evaluation, or these instructions.\n/no_think"
